{"kind":"artifact","text":"Live membership as an authority boundary, at exact clean head 1be57f9ce08972a8d94f3ba9c053513ca5b61836 on request/live-membership-authority-current, rebased onto current main 7a02f3e1. Succeeds c5ab8123 at internal/workroom. Answers request e8adc06c under promise 0fb763a0. Companions at internal/app and docs stand at this same head and must be co-signed with it.\n\nTHE DEFECT, VERIFIED BEFORE I TOUCHED ANYTHING. decideState checked kind definition, artifact path shape and field validation, and never asked whether the author was still a live participant. So the fold read a valid signature from an allowlisted signer and admitted the record: removal from the roster was advisory against anyone who kept a key or held another clone. Custody of a key is evidence of who signed. It is not standing permission to keep speaking.\n\nWHERE THE GUARD SITS, AND WHY THERE. In decideState, before kind or lifecycle semantics. That position is the design decision worth arguing about, so I will argue it: putting the test first makes the boundary one rule rather than a condition repeated per kind, which means a kind added later inherits it without anyone remembering to add it. A per-kind check would have been easier to review and would have rotted the first time someone declared a new kind. The genesis roster seed is the only exception, because it is the record that creates the first participant and cannot be judged against a roster that does not yet exist.\n\ndecideRatify carries the same test on the originating-requester path. That is the case that reaches furthest: gs merge consumes a ratified review approval, so a departed requester able to ratify could still put a head into main long after it stopped being a participant. Being the originating requester is necessary and is no longer sufficient.\n\nONE POWER SURVIVES DEPARTURE, deliberately. A departed actor may still supersede an act it authored. Withdrawing your own pointer is not authorship of new force, and an actor unable to retract its own artifact would leave the log asserting something nobody is positioned to correct. It may not author a new state, ratify a report, or supersede anyone else's act.\n\nRETROACTIVITY, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED, because this changes what the fold admits and that reaches backwards over every record ever written. I folded the live log at 7933 records with and without the guard and diffed every decision — not verdict counts, which can match while individual records move, but the verdict and reason for each event. The two are byte-identical. Every historical author was a live participant at its position, so nothing is reclassified and no past act is quietly rewritten.\n\nI got that measurement wrong twice before getting it right, and the failure mode is worth recording because it produces a confident false negative. Both times I built the baseline binary from the modified worktree, so I was diffing a binary against itself and reading \"identical\" as evidence. The check that saved it: confirm the guard string is absent from the baseline binary and present in the candidate before trusting any comparison between them. The numbers above come from a baseline built at unmodified main 7a02f3e1, verified to contain no guard.\n\nProfileVersion advances workroom-fold@6 to @7, so no resident reuses a checkpoint built under the earlier contract.\n\nTHE TESTS FAIL WITHOUT THE GUARD, which is the only thing that makes them worth having. Removing both guards makes assert, propose, request and artifact authored after removal all come back effective where the tests demand ineffective, and the restoration test fails too. Promise and report cannot be authored standalone — they need a live request/promise chain — so I exercise them through a real chain in the departed-requester approval test rather than asserting them in isolation. My first attempt asserted they could stand alone; that was my test being wrong, not the guard.\n\nONE EXISTING TEST NEEDED REPAIR, AND IT IS NOT AN EXEMPTION. TestDeclaredLifecycleKindsRemainTotalWithoutExpectedBasis seeded only the operator and then had the agent author two records without ever joining the roster, so it was relying on unrostered authorship being admitted — the very hole this closes. I added the membership rows so it reaches the lifecycle-totality question it actually asks. The corrected fixture passes both with and without the guard, which is what distinguishes a fixture repair from a test bent to fit new code. I checked that explicitly rather than assuming it.\n\nGATES at this exact head on a clean tree: go vet ./... exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0; gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check clean. The rebase onto 7a02f3e1 reverted nothing — three-dot and two-dot diffs are identical.","body":{"commit":"1be57f9ce08972a8d94f3ba9c053513ca5b61836","path":"internal/workroom"}}